WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:08.090
Well, everyone, thanks
for coming to my talk.

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:09.080
My name is June.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.590
I'm a PhD student in
linguistics at the University

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:12.660
of Pennsylvania.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.580
And today I'll be talking about
ggplot internals and my package

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.030
ggtrace.

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:19.850
So I suspect that most
of us here are already

00:00:19.850 --> 00:00:21.265
pretty familiar with ggplot.

00:00:21.265 --> 00:00:23.390
But just to get a sense of
the room, how many of us

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:26.598
has made like a box
plot in a ggplot before?

00:00:26.598 --> 00:00:28.390
All right, that's like,
literally everyone.

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:30.340
Cool and I make them
all the time, too.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.230
It's like one of the first plots
I make when I get results back

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:34.470
from an experiment.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:37.650
So let's say like a behavioral
data of, like, button presses.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:40.070
So each row is a response
from a participant

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:42.140
and there's columns for
experiment, condition

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:43.350
and response time.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.140
So I can write a
short code where

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:48.710
I pass my data to ggplot,
map condition to x and fill,

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:52.070
and response times to y
and add a geom_boxplot layer,

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:54.690
pretty straightforward
that gives me a box plot.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:58.250
So that was very
simple to do in ggplot.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:01.310
And oftentimes I'll have
participants who are behaving,

00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:03.650
let's say, more
interestingly than others,

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:06.840
and I care about identifying
them for a lot of reasons.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.010
So I might also want to
annotate the cutoff value

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:12.320
for extreme outliers like this.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.962
And of course I should calculate
it myself if I really care.

00:01:14.962 --> 00:01:16.170
And if it's really important.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:19.040
But I'm just doing a
quick exploratory analysis

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.220
and I already know how to
tell ggplot to calculate

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:22.980
this for me.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.600
So labeling that same value
should be a trivial task,

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:28.150
right?

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:30.970
Except it turns out this is kind
of tricky because what we're

00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:34.390
trying to do is do something
like a box plot layer,

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:38.380
except divert its normal course
of action under the hood.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:41.560
So our new layer should
calculate the same variable

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:42.770
as our first layer.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:45.160
But then instead of
using boxes and whiskers

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:48.490
to draw all of them, it
should label just one of them.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:50.320
And when it comes
to these situations

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.230
where the user wants to
intervene in the process

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:54.790
of how a layer comes to life,

00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:56.950
there is good news and bad news.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:59.350
The good news is, thanks
to recent developments,

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:04.300
we can write a layer that does
all inside and all on the fly,

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:05.930
all inside a geom_label layer.

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:07.220
So that's great.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.889
But the bad news is this
is incomprehensible.

00:02:09.889 --> 00:02:12.230
Like this is not your
typical ggplot code.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:14.110
So we have strange
functions called

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:16.540
inside the aes like
stage and after_stat

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:20.050
and we're using the stat and
data arguments of the geom_label

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:22.010
layer, which we don't often do.

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.820
So like this is still
like vanilla ggplot,

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:27.700
but this kind of code, it's
very difficult to reason about

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:29.710
because it requires
a mental model

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:31.540
of how ggplot internals work.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:33.790
And here's what I mean by that.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:36.070
So the code for our
first layer is easy

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:38.620
because we can get away
with thinking about a layer

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.610
as just doing one big thing.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:42.880
So like, hey, geom_box plot, 
here's

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:45.520
all the stuff you need
now go do your thing.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.210
But we need to revise the simple
model of how layers are built

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:52.540
to understand code, like the
second layer, which reads more

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:56.200
like, hey layer, you know that data
that I gave you to draw, apply

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:57.940
this function to
it first and then

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:01.180
proceed as normal with condition
mapped to x and response times

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:02.050
mapped to y.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:05.500
Then after the box plots
that steps in and transforms

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:08.560
the data, then use the
computed variable y-max

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.900
to remap to the y
aesthetic and do the same

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:12.080
for the label aesthetic.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.360
And then lastly, use the
label geom to draw what you have.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:16.270
That was a mouthful.

00:03:16.270 --> 00:03:17.320
And you get the point.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:21.180
What appear to be a simple
task of annotating a box plot

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:21.970
variable

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:24.990
turns out to require a
deep understanding of what

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:27.035
goes on where in the internals.

00:03:27.035 --> 00:03:28.410
And it's not just
because there's

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:31.620
more code that is difficult.
It's conceptually difficult

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.260
because it makes references
to different steps

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.230
in the internals that we
as users don't get to see.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:38.833
And I don't know
about you, but I

00:03:38.833 --> 00:03:40.750
would love to be able
to write code like this.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.080
So if a knowledge,
if it requires

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.840
the knowledge of the internals,
if that's what it takes,

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.930
then maybe ggplot internals
aren't just for developers

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:49.680
anymore.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:51.840
And so that's what I
want to talk about today.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.900
I would like to propose
a model of ggplot internals

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.990
for users, one that's
immediately practical to users

00:03:57.990 --> 00:03:59.730
just wanting to
get better at ggplot,

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:01.860
but also one that
offers an accessible

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.470
starting point for
aspiring developers who

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:06.940
want to extend ggplot.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:09.020
So here's my proposal
in a nutshell,

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:12.040
why don't we just pretend that
ggplot internals is one

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:13.520
big data wrangling function?

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:14.870
Because it kind of is.

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:18.130
And to do that, I have developed
my package ggtrace,

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:20.140
which I'll be showcasing today.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:23.110
So here is the outline
for the rest of my talk.

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:26.110
So first, I'll introduce
this reframing of ggplot

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:28.060
internals as data
wrangling, which

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:30.970
will also provide context and
motivation for my package

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:31.750
ggtrace.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:35.230
And then I'll demonstrate how we
can put this idea into practice

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:38.710
using a simple example using
functions from ggtrace,

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:41.080
starting with an
example of a bar plot

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:44.050
and then returning to our
box plot annotation problem

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:46.870
and to building up the
solution from scratch.

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:49.570
So our dive into
the internals begins

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:53.380
with this factual observation
that each layer of a ggplot

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:56.150
has an underlying data
frame representation.

00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:58.270
So in other words,
layers are just data

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:00.310
frames until they get drawn.

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:03.730
So we can actually see this
using ggplot's own layer data

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:04.540
function.

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:07.300
So we pass it our plot
and the index of the layer

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:08.330
we want to inspect.

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:11.260
And it gives us the data
that's underlying the box plot

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:12.320
layer of our plot.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:14.500
And this is the
actual data frame

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:18.340
that gets sent off to the quote
unquote drawing system, which

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:21.190
is kind of the graphical object
land with like grid functions

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:22.550
and table functions.

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:24.460
But for our purposes,
we'll just pretend

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:28.192
that all of the internals is
a function that just takes

00:05:28.192 --> 00:05:30.400
the raw data that we give
it, which is the experiment

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:34.300
data that we provided and makes
it drawing ready like this

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.510
and does this process for
every layer in our plot

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:40.390
according to each layer's
own specifications.

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:42.830
And this doesn't
look too bad, right?

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:45.520
Our input was a tidy
data and the output

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:48.700
is just another tidy data
where each row represents

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:51.790
a box plot and the columns
are aesthetics that describe

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:55.210
each box plot, including the
value for the upper whiskers

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:57.160
stored in the y-max column.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:59.410
And crucially, as
you might suspect,

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:02.320
this process of making a
layer of data drawing ready

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:05.120
happens in steps
not all at once.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:07.690
So why do we care
about that as users?

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:09.730
Well, because, again,
different pieces

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:13.240
of the code that we write
also kicks in different steps,

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:16.720
step by step, to make the
layers data more and more

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:17.720
drawing ready.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:19.960
So it's literally
like data wrangling

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:22.150
happening under the
hood, except you

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:24.340
don't get to see your
data again after it

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:26.800
goes off and off your hands.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:28.690
You know, so kind of like this.

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:31.010
So I'm like simplifying
things a bit here.

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:34.060
But for our purposes as users,
we can think of the internals

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:38.080
as divided up or sliced into
these four big steps, which

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:41.680
I'll call the before
stat, the Before Geom,

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:44.950
or, Before Stat, After Stat,
Before Geom and After Scale.

00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:47.650
And because they
happen in order,

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:50.500
how a piece of our
code, layer code,

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:53.710
changes the current state of
a data in this pipeline at one

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:56.110
step has consequences
for what kind of code

00:06:56.110 --> 00:06:59.050
we can write for that
data in another step.

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:00.970
So, so far, so good.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:03.550
Now we just need to see
what the data actually

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:05.060
looks like at these steps.

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:07.300
So we go digging
into the internals

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:10.270
and we find that the data passes
through these scary-looking

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:12.420
things called ggproto
methods and not

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:14.920
like the kind of dplyr of
functions we're familiar with

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:17.680
and the four steps that
we care about as users,

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:21.370
those are also the inputs and
outputs of these ggproto methods,

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:22.880
these three to be exact.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:24.430
So in the internals,
the raw data

00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:27.910
is input and the Before Stat
data is the state of the layer

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:30.420
data when it's passed
into the layer

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:32.950
ggproto object's compute
statistic method

00:07:32.950 --> 00:07:36.190
that the After Stat data is
the output of that same method

00:07:36.190 --> 00:07:39.528
and then the Before Geom data is
the input to this next method

00:07:39.528 --> 00:07:41.320
and then the After
Scale data is the output

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:42.290
of this other method.

00:07:42.290 --> 00:07:43.660
And then some more
stuff happens and then

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:45.327
the data becomes
drawing ready, and then

00:07:45.327 --> 00:07:46.990
it gets sent off to be drawn.

00:07:46.990 --> 00:07:49.510
So the fact that the
internals are written this way

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:51.490
is a big reason why
it's deliberately

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:53.180
kept hidden from us users.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:55.990
ggproto is scary
and we're honestly

00:07:55.990 --> 00:08:00.160
like better off not knowing
anything about them.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:03.320
Like this first method here,
Layer$compute_statistic.

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:06.380
It looks like this and it's
like nothing you've seen before.

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:09.170
It's like a function, but it's
composed two other functions,

00:08:09.170 --> 00:08:10.960
and it has this
argument called self,

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:13.460
which is like a big thing from
object-oriented programming.

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:15.830
That's like very intimidating
to me as an R user.

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:18.550
And maybe to you too, although
if you are interested in OOP,

00:08:18.550 --> 00:08:21.830
you're in the right place
just like 40 minutes earlier.

00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:24.320
And this method is actually
also like a nested call

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:26.000
to several other ggproto
methods and they're

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:29.370
like all written in base R and
it can get very overwhelming.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:32.809
And recently they also added
in vectors integration and CLI

00:08:32.809 --> 00:08:35.330
integration and
it's a lot like, OK,

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:37.610
I wanted to know what
goes on in the internals,

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:38.900
but not like this.

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:42.570
This is developer
territory, right?

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:45.300
All I care about is
just seeing the data.

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:46.940
Like give me the
data frame so that I

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:49.190
can anticipate what
my code will do

00:08:49.190 --> 00:08:51.680
in the middle of the
pipeline when it kicks in.

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:54.170
And so that's the motivation
behind ggtrace.

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:57.290
So ggtrace is a package
that allows us, the users,

00:08:57.290 --> 00:08:59.600
to drop inside
like parachute down

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:02.930
this internal pipeline at any
point we want and intercept

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:05.060
the data at that
step and interact

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:07.460
with it using the kind
of data wrangling tools

00:09:07.460 --> 00:09:09.380
that we're already
familiar with.

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:11.330
So ggtrace
comes with a family

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:14.510
of workflow functions in the
form of ggtrace_{action}_{value}(),

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:17.930
which all take
three arguments: the ggplot

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:20.660
object, the ggproto
method and when

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:22.890
to interact with that method.

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:25.080
So this talk will showcase
just two functions

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:27.810
from the inspect workflow,
ggtrace_inspect_args()

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:29.970
and ggtrace_inspect_return(),
which

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:32.940
lets us take a snapshot
of a layer's data

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:36.520
as it goes in and
out of ggproto methods.

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:38.170
So for example,
if we want to look

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:41.710
at the state of a layer of
data in the Before Stat stage,

00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:45.820
then we can use ggtrace_inspect_args()
to intercept the data that

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:48.370
was passed into their
compute statistic

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:50.290
when it is first
called for our plot,

00:09:50.290 --> 00:09:54.220
which gives us the box
plot layers After Stat data

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:56.812
and then likewise if we want to
look at the layers after that

00:09:56.812 --> 00:09:58.520
or sorry, that was
the Before Stat data.

00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:00.270
If we want to look at
the After Stat data,

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:03.010
we need to use
ggtrace_inspect_return() instead

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:05.320
to intercept the data
when it is returned

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:07.810
by that same method, which
gives us the box plot

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:09.930
layers After Stat data.

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:11.880
So now that we're
equipped with the tools

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:14.490
to peek inside the
internals, let's use

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:18.270
these inspect functions to walk
through how a layer gets built

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:20.700
for the bar plot, step
by step, and we'll

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:22.390
be using the Penguins data set.

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:24.390
So if you're not
familiar, this is a data

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:28.140
set where each row is a penguin
with columns per species

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:29.400
and bill length.

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:32.520
And so using geom_bar we
can visualize the count

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:35.820
of penguins in each species
by mapping the species column

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:36.970
to x and fill.

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:38.010
So simple, right?

00:10:38.010 --> 00:10:39.517
Pretty straightforward.

00:10:39.517 --> 00:10:42.100
We don't really think about how
layers are built in steps when

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:46.090
we write code like this, but
once we spell out the defaults

00:10:46.090 --> 00:10:49.020
like stat equals count and
y equals after_stat count,

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:51.940
we see clear parallels to
our more complex box plot

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:54.890
annotation layer, which is
why I like this example.

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:57.020
So in the internals,
the bar labels data

00:10:57.020 --> 00:10:59.390
starts off as the
Penguins data and ends up

00:10:59.390 --> 00:11:00.720
in its drawing ready form.

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:02.420
And to see how this
all happens, we

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:05.600
use ggtrace to intercept
the data at the four steps

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:07.210
that we care about.

00:11:07.210 --> 00:11:09.070
So let's look at
some data frames.

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:12.440
We start by intercepting the
data at the Before Stat stage.

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:14.440
So we use ggtrace_inspect_args()
again

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:16.150
to pull out the
data argument that

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:19.120
was passed into this ggproto method,
which gives us a data

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:20.680
frame that looks like this.

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:22.810
So here our initial
aesthetic mappings

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:25.390
are reflected in the presence
of these columns named

00:11:25.390 --> 00:11:27.670
after the actual
aesthetics like x and fill,

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:29.385
just like basically
select and rename.

00:11:29.385 --> 00:11:30.760
And we see some
other things that

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:33.830
happen to the data at this point
as well, like the x is numeric.

00:11:33.830 --> 00:11:36.053
And we have these new
columns like PANEL and group.

00:11:36.053 --> 00:11:38.470
If we pass the function to the
data argument of the layer,

00:11:38.470 --> 00:11:40.420
this is also the
point by which that

00:11:40.420 --> 00:11:42.010
would get applied to our data.

00:11:42.010 --> 00:11:44.860
But the real significance
of the Before Stat data

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:47.350
is that it validates the
layers choice of stat.

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:49.900
So our Before Stat
data has a column for x

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:52.810
And that satisfies the
layers count stat, which

00:11:52.810 --> 00:11:56.080
requires either an x
or y aesthetic as we

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:58.340
see from the documentation.

00:11:58.340 --> 00:12:00.620
If it's specified both
x and y like this,

00:12:00.620 --> 00:12:03.770
then the plot fails to build
and it errors specifically

00:12:03.770 --> 00:12:06.080
at stat_count.

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:09.200
And so we can set error equals
true in our ggtrace function

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:10.830
to debug the layers data.

00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:12.900
And here we can
see the reason why.

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:14.960
It's because both
the columns x and y

00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:18.930
are present when the stat
goes in to look at the data.

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:20.648
And if we...

00:12:20.648 --> 00:12:22.440
But as long as the
stat has what it needs,

00:12:22.440 --> 00:12:23.982
then it will transform
the data, spit

00:12:23.982 --> 00:12:26.940
it back out for us to see
in the After Stat stage.

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:29.790
So at this point, we see
that the data basically

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:31.720
underwent a group_by() and
summarize().

00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:34.530
So we have one row for
each bar and new variables

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:35.730
like count and prop.

00:12:35.730 --> 00:12:38.460
So the After Stat stage
is significant because it's

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:41.700
an opportunity for us to
declare more aesthetic mappings,

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:45.810
except we can use variables
from this After Stat dataframe.

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:48.300
So the fact that we have a
column called count in the

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:51.660
After Stat data is what allows
this default mapping of y

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:54.780
equals after_stat(count) and
that's essentially just calling

00:12:54.780 --> 00:12:57.330
mutate(y = count)
on the After Stat data.

00:12:57.330 --> 00:12:58.830
And like this isn't
just a metaphor,

00:12:58.830 --> 00:13:01.690
like aesthetic mappings are
literally powered by tidy eval.

00:13:01.690 --> 00:13:04.200
So the symbol count is
evaluated to the vector count.

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:06.750
So you can do things
like y equals count

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:09.300
over sum of count, which will
give you proportions and have

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:11.340
consequences down the
line for the plot.

00:13:11.340 --> 00:13:13.080
And any After Stat
mappings that we

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:15.690
declare like this will get
applied to the data for us

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:19.500
to see by the time the data
reaches the Before Geom stage.

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:22.560
So we see now the data
has that y column present

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:24.000
when we intercept it here.

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:26.040
And just like in
the Before Stat

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:30.060
the Before Geom's
data validates the

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:31.420
layer's choice of geometry.

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:34.530
So here we have both x
and y columns present

00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:38.070
and that satisfies our
layer's geom, which is geom_bar,

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:41.130
and it requires both
x and y aesthetics.

00:13:41.130 --> 00:13:42.960
If we instead
overwrite this default

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:46.110
and not map anything to y at
all by setting it to null,

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:47.800
then the stat is satisfied.

00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:50.410
But the geom down
the line is not so,

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:52.890
it's geom_bar that
draws the error.

00:13:52.890 --> 00:13:54.900
And so conceptually
we can see how

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:56.670
After Scale mappings
are sometimes

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:59.310
necessary to make a
stat and a geom work.

00:13:59.310 --> 00:14:03.180
So every layer has a stat in a
geom and they need to fit together

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:05.580
to make a layer come to life.

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:07.770
So the next time that
we intercept the data

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:10.080
is in the After Scale stage.

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:12.630
So by this point the
non-positional scales

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:15.090
have stepped in to transform
aesthetics like fill,

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:17.820
which is now a column of the
actual color values instead

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:19.690
of the names of the
penguin species.

00:14:19.690 --> 00:14:21.690
We also see that the
bar geom has stepped

00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:24.600
in to add default values
for bar-related aesthetics,

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:27.130
which are again just
columns like color and size.

00:14:27.130 --> 00:14:29.790
This stage is significant
because just like the After

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:32.640
Stat, we can declare
more aesthetic mappings

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:35.310
one last time before the
data sent off to be drawn.

00:14:35.310 --> 00:14:37.800
So for example, we have the
size column by this point

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:41.010
representing the thickness of
the borders around the bars.

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:43.480
It's actually kind of hard
to tell the default value.

00:14:43.480 --> 00:14:44.230
It's small.

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:46.530
So if we wanted to make
it like five times thicker

00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:49.170
than it was originally going to
be, then you can kind of just

00:14:49.170 --> 00:14:51.480
do that on the fly,
grab the size column

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:55.140
when it exists in the After
Scale data, multiply it by 5

00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:58.900
and map it or assign it
back to the size aesthetic.

00:14:58.900 --> 00:15:00.930
And it's kind of like so
aesthetic mappings are

00:15:00.930 --> 00:15:04.500
kind of like scheduling a mutate()
call on your data for later.

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:06.870
And this can happen on
the fly without us needing

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:09.930
to figure out the default value
first and then for the data

00:15:09.930 --> 00:15:10.930
viz folks in the room.

00:15:10.930 --> 00:15:12.347
This is also what
allows you to do

00:15:12.347 --> 00:15:14.580
stuff make the outline
of the bars a little

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:16.150
bit darker than the
fill at the bars,

00:15:16.150 --> 00:15:18.150
and then that can make
your plots look prettier.

00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:19.525
I'll just leave
this here for you

00:15:19.525 --> 00:15:21.260
if you want to look at it later.

00:15:21.260 --> 00:15:23.670
So that concludes our
bar plot walkthrough.

00:15:23.670 --> 00:15:25.850
So to recap, a layer
of data becomes

00:15:25.850 --> 00:15:28.580
drawing ready in
steps in the internals

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:31.050
and using the inspect
function from ggtrace,

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:33.470
we were able to see
what the data looks

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:35.810
like at a certain
steps and crucially

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:39.380
how the state of the layers data
can inform what kind of layer

00:15:39.380 --> 00:15:40.820
code that we can write.

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:43.880
And so with that we circle
back to our box plot annotation

00:15:43.880 --> 00:15:45.440
layer.

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:48.020
Again, we have this box
plot of response times

00:15:48.020 --> 00:15:50.030
by condition that we
made at the beginning,

00:15:50.030 --> 00:15:51.950
and we would like to
add a layer labeling

00:15:51.950 --> 00:15:54.740
the value of the upper whisker
in the second box plot.

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:56.540
And so applying what
we just learned,

00:15:56.540 --> 00:15:59.450
we can write this layer in
steps, just like how the layer

00:15:59.450 --> 00:16:01.250
itself is built up in steps.

00:16:01.250 --> 00:16:03.050
So this is kind of a
hard code, so we'll

00:16:03.050 --> 00:16:04.633
get stuck at some
point, but then I'll

00:16:04.633 --> 00:16:07.640
show you how we can use ggtrace
to debug our way out of it.

00:16:07.640 --> 00:16:10.460
So first thing we start is
a high-level description

00:16:10.460 --> 00:16:11.410
of what we want.

00:16:11.410 --> 00:16:13.540
So every layer has
a stat and a geom.

00:16:13.540 --> 00:16:14.700
So maybe let's start there.

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:19.320
We want to use a label to draw
a box plot variable. In code,

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:22.280
that means geom_label with
stat equals boxplot

00:16:22.280 --> 00:16:25.220
or stat_boxplot
with geom equals label.

00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:27.380
Again, every layer needs
both and it's actually

00:16:27.380 --> 00:16:28.710
your choice of syntax.

00:16:28.710 --> 00:16:30.240
They're the same thing.

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:33.300
And next we can specify the data
that we want the layer to plot.

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:35.280
So we only care about
the second box plot.

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:39.470
We say filter for condition
is B and let that apply.

00:16:39.470 --> 00:16:41.510
Then we build up the
aesthetic mappings,

00:16:41.510 --> 00:16:43.820
starting with the ones
that the stat needs first

00:16:43.820 --> 00:16:46.070
because the stat always
receives the data first,

00:16:46.070 --> 00:16:49.400
so we specify the x and y
And then additional ones

00:16:49.400 --> 00:16:51.680
that the geom might
need later like label,

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:53.720
which we want to be
the same as the y-max

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:56.217
value that the stat computes.

00:16:56.217 --> 00:16:58.050
And so that gives us
this big chunk of code,

00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:00.540
and we're almost there
except for this error

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:03.060
that says geom_label is
missing a y-aesthetic.

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:05.138
And, like what, we do
have a y-aesthetic here.

00:17:05.138 --> 00:17:06.930
So like, maybe we're
not sure what's wrong,

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:09.079
but we know how to debug this.

00:17:09.079 --> 00:17:11.790
The geom is complaining
about a missing aesthetic,

00:17:11.790 --> 00:17:15.119
and so something must be wrong
with the data that it receives.

00:17:15.119 --> 00:17:19.140
So then we inspect the Before
Geom snapshot of the layers data,

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:21.950
and we do see that the y-
column has been dropped.

00:17:21.950 --> 00:17:23.730
So it turns out that
the box plots stat

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:26.280
consumes the y-column
and then returns

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:28.480
a summary across multiple
columns like this.

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:30.330
So the y-column is missing now.

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:33.190
So to resolve that, we can
use the stage function again.

00:17:33.190 --> 00:17:36.870
Just this is a ggplot function,
stage function, to map y

00:17:36.870 --> 00:17:38.700
at the start, to
satisfy the stat

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:41.520
and then remap to y
in the After Stat

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:45.330
to satisfy the geom. And then that
gets us the layer that we want

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:48.240
because this ensures that
both the Before Stat

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:52.380
data and the Before Geom
data has a y-column present.

00:17:52.380 --> 00:17:55.310
So then that's what finally
gets the layer working.

00:17:55.310 --> 00:17:59.990
So that concludes cracking open
ggplot internals with ggtrace.

00:17:59.990 --> 00:18:02.310
Again, ggtrace is helpful.

00:18:02.310 --> 00:18:04.400
It helps us learn the
internals as users

00:18:04.400 --> 00:18:07.130
by exploiting the fact
that a lot of the internals

00:18:07.130 --> 00:18:10.820
is just manipulating data frames
and by reframing the internals

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:13.730
into familiar data wrangling,
functional programming terms

00:18:13.730 --> 00:18:16.400
that we're all familiar
with, we arrive

00:18:16.400 --> 00:18:19.290
at a conceptual understanding
of how the internals work.

00:18:19.290 --> 00:18:23.060
So this lets us write more
powerful ggplot code as users,

00:18:23.060 --> 00:18:25.700
while still mostly
abstracting away

00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:28.700
from the scary implementation
details like ggproto and base

00:18:28.700 --> 00:18:29.640
R functions.

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:31.490
But if you are
interested in that,

00:18:31.490 --> 00:18:34.023
ggtrace has other workflow
functions that you can use.

00:18:34.023 --> 00:18:35.690
So just really quickly,
there's function

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:38.540
from the capture workflow
like ggtrace_capture_fn(),

00:18:38.540 --> 00:18:41.540
which essentially records
the behavior of a ggproto method

00:18:41.540 --> 00:18:42.930
when it executes.

00:18:42.930 --> 00:18:45.440
And then you can kind of have
it as a standalone function.

00:18:45.440 --> 00:18:48.680
You have functions from
the highjack workflow

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:51.710
which kind of lets you
pass or make ggproto methods,

00:18:51.710 --> 00:18:53.160
return arbitrary values.

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:55.160
And so you can kind of
try to hack the internals

00:18:55.160 --> 00:18:58.920
and see what you can come
up with and yeah, that's it.

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:00.050
ggtrace is on GitHub.

00:19:00.050 --> 00:19:01.550
Here are some links,
other materials

00:19:01.550 --> 00:19:04.120
and I'd love to
hear your feedback.

